1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,173
A blocked, runny nose.

2
00:00:02,173 --> 00:00:04,742
A tobacco leaf with a patchy pattern.

3
00:00:04,742 --> 00:00:06,566
A bacterium bursting open.

4
00:00:06,966 --> 00:00:08,816
Three very different victims.

5
00:00:08,816 --> 00:00:11,953
One kind of attacker, far
smaller than any cell.

6
00:00:12,403 --> 00:00:15,939
Diya had a bad cold and
asked for an antibiotic.

7
00:00:15,939 --> 00:00:17,505
The doctor said no.

8
00:00:17,505 --> 00:00:21,987
Antibiotics act on bacteria,
and a cold is caused by a virus.

9
00:00:22,337 --> 00:00:25,115
So what is a virus, and is it even alive?

10
00:00:25,115 --> 00:00:26,162
Let's find out.

11
00:00:26,812 --> 00:00:28,854
First, how were viruses found?

12
00:00:28,854 --> 00:00:30,839
Three scientists, three steps.

13
00:00:31,189 --> 00:00:35,615
In eighteen ninety two, Dmitri
Ivanowsky studied the mosaic disease of

14
00:00:35,615 --> 00:00:36,515
tobacco.

15
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:39,764
Its cause passed through
filters that stop bacteria.

16
00:00:39,764 --> 00:00:42,033
So it was smaller than bacteria.

17
00:00:42,483 --> 00:00:47,249
In eighteen ninety eight, MW Beijerinek
showed that juice from an infected

18
00:00:47,249 --> 00:00:49,550
plant could infect a healthy one.

19
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:53,613
He called it contagium vivum
fluidum: infectious living fluid.

20
00:00:54,063 --> 00:00:59,173
In nineteen thirty five, WM Stanley
showed that viruses could be crystallised,

21
00:00:59,173 --> 00:01:01,697
and the crystals were mostly protein.

22
00:01:02,347 --> 00:01:03,959
So, is a virus alive?

23
00:01:04,309 --> 00:01:06,763
Outside a cell, a virus is inert.

24
00:01:06,763 --> 00:01:08,385
It is like a crystal.

25
00:01:08,385 --> 00:01:10,271
It does not feed or grow.

26
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:15,303
Inside a cell, it takes over the cell's
machinery to make copies of itself,

27
00:01:15,303 --> 00:01:16,527
killing the host.

28
00:01:16,977 --> 00:01:22,123
So viruses are obligate parasites: they
multiply only inside a living host.

29
00:01:22,123 --> 00:01:26,308
Not made of cells, they fit
in none of the five kingdoms.

30
00:01:26,958 --> 00:01:30,861
Now let's zoom into the sick
tobacco leaf, one level at a time.

31
00:01:31,211 --> 00:01:34,784
The leaf shows a mosaic:
light and dark green patches.

32
00:01:35,284 --> 00:01:36,184
Zoom in.

33
00:01:36,141 --> 00:01:40,192
Inside each leaf cell,
rod-shaped viruses are piling up.

34
00:01:40,692 --> 00:01:42,252
Zoom into one rod.

35
00:01:42,252 --> 00:01:44,787
This is the tobacco mosaic virus.

36
00:01:44,787 --> 00:01:49,645
Its protein coat is the capsid,
and inside runs a strand of RNA.

37
00:01:50,145 --> 00:01:51,128
Zoom again.

38
00:01:51,128 --> 00:01:55,891
The capsid is built from small protein
subunits called capsomeres, packed

39
00:01:55,891 --> 00:01:57,984
in a spiral around the RNA.

40
00:01:58,634 --> 00:02:00,788
So every virus has two parts.

41
00:02:01,138 --> 00:02:04,716
Inside is the genetic material,
the set of instructions.

42
00:02:04,716 --> 00:02:07,450
Outside is the capsid, which protects it.

43
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:13,084
Capsomeres can be arranged in a
helical, or spiral form, like this rod,

44
00:02:13,084 --> 00:02:15,809
or a polyhedral, or many-faced form.

45
00:02:16,259 --> 00:02:19,765
The genetic material is either RNA or DNA.

46
00:02:19,765 --> 00:02:21,796
No virus contains both.

47
00:02:21,796 --> 00:02:26,504
So a virus is a nucleoprotein:
nucleic acid plus protein.

48
00:02:27,154 --> 00:02:31,707
Now watch a bacteriophage, a virus
of bacteria, take over a cell.

49
00:02:32,057 --> 00:02:32,957
Step one.

50
00:02:32,916 --> 00:02:37,505
The phage lands on the bacterium
and holds on with its tail fibres.

51
00:02:38,255 --> 00:02:39,157
Step two.

52
00:02:39,157 --> 00:02:41,712
It injects its DNA into the cell.

53
00:02:41,712 --> 00:02:44,420
The empty protein coat stays outside.

54
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:46,217
Step three.

55
00:02:46,217 --> 00:02:51,566
The bacterium treats the viral DNA as
if it were its own, and makes viral

56
00:02:51,566 --> 00:02:53,433
DNA and viral proteins.

57
00:02:54,183 --> 00:02:55,137
Step four.

58
00:02:55,137 --> 00:02:58,174
These parts assemble into many new phages.

59
00:02:58,924 --> 00:02:59,824
Step five.

60
00:02:59,816 --> 00:03:04,839
The cell bursts and dies, and the new
phages go out to infect more cells.

61
00:03:05,589 --> 00:03:08,475
Which nucleic acid does each virus carry?

62
00:03:08,825 --> 00:03:14,357
Viruses that infect plants usually have
single stranded RNA, like the tobacco

63
00:03:14,357 --> 00:03:15,553
mosaic virus.

64
00:03:16,003 --> 00:03:20,374
Bacteriophages are usually
double stranded DNA viruses.

65
00:03:20,824 --> 00:03:26,161
Viruses that infect animals have single
or double stranded RNA, or double

66
00:03:26,161 --> 00:03:27,366
stranded DNA.

67
00:03:28,016 --> 00:03:28,945
Your turn.

68
00:03:28,945 --> 00:03:32,243
A virus is found to carry
double stranded DNA.

69
00:03:32,243 --> 00:03:36,033
Is it more likely a plant
virus, or a bacteriophage?

70
00:03:36,033 --> 00:03:37,400
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:42,028
A bacteriophage!

72
00:03:42,028 --> 00:03:45,776
Plant viruses usually
carry single stranded RNA.

73
00:03:46,426 --> 00:03:48,301
And the damage viruses do?

74
00:03:48,651 --> 00:03:53,661
In humans, viruses cause mumps, small
pox, herpes, influenza and AIDS.

75
00:03:53,661 --> 00:03:58,984
In plants: mosaic patches, leaf rolling
and curling, yellowing, vein clearing,

76
00:03:58,984 --> 00:04:00,422
and stunted growth.

77
00:04:01,072 --> 00:04:02,394
Remember this.

78
00:04:02,394 --> 00:04:06,163
A virus is RNA or DNA
inside a protein coat.

79
00:04:06,163 --> 00:04:07,300
Never both.

80
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:09,561
Now a worked example.

81
00:04:09,561 --> 00:04:12,226
Let's label two viruses side by side.

82
00:04:12,576 --> 00:04:15,741
The tobacco mosaic virus is a helical rod.

83
00:04:15,741 --> 00:04:18,795
Its capsid wraps a single strand of RNA.

84
00:04:18,795 --> 00:04:20,528
Its host is a plant.

85
00:04:20,978 --> 00:04:25,565
The bacteriophage has a many-faced
head, packed with double stranded DNA.

86
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:29,305
Below it are the collar, a
tail sheath and tail fibres.

87
00:04:29,755 --> 00:04:31,484
Its host is a bacterium.

88
00:04:31,484 --> 00:04:35,386
Different shapes, but both are
nucleic acid inside a capsid.

89
00:04:36,036 --> 00:04:37,502
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:37,852 --> 00:04:42,776
Many students say a virus has both
DNA and RNA, just like a cell.

91
00:04:43,176 --> 00:04:45,930
But a virus carries only one kind.

92
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:48,499
Either RNA or DNA, never both.

93
00:04:49,149 --> 00:04:50,689
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:50,689 --> 00:04:55,353
NEET asks statement questions on
viruses: which line about their structure

95
00:04:55,353 --> 00:04:57,369
or genetic material is true.

96
00:04:57,969 --> 00:04:59,170
Exam level now.

97
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:03,273
Match each name in list one
with the right idea in list two.

98
00:05:03,273 --> 00:05:04,525
Pause and solve.

99
00:05:09,925 --> 00:05:11,286
The answer is A.

100
00:05:11,786 --> 00:05:16,523
Ivanowsky found the tobacco mosaic
agent was smaller than bacteria.

101
00:05:16,523 --> 00:05:19,749
Beijerinek called it
contagium vivum fluidum.

102
00:05:20,249 --> 00:05:23,916
Stanley crystallised viruses
and found them mostly protein.

103
00:05:23,916 --> 00:05:26,935
And capsomeres are the
subunits of the capsid.

104
00:05:27,535 --> 00:05:28,993
Here is your recap.

105
00:05:28,993 --> 00:05:30,493
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:33,693 --> 00:05:36,529
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:05:36,929 --> 00:05:37,829
One.

108
00:05:37,488 --> 00:05:40,642
Why is a virus called
an obligate parasite?

109
00:05:41,542 --> 00:05:42,442
Two.

110
00:05:41,974 --> 00:05:44,514
What is a capsid, and what is it made of?

111
00:05:45,414 --> 00:05:46,314
Three.

112
00:05:46,038 --> 00:05:48,505
What did Stanley show about viruses?

113
00:05:49,605 --> 00:05:54,252
Next time: something smaller than a
virus, with no protein coat at all,

114
00:05:54,252 --> 00:05:55,930
still makes plants sick.

115
00:05:55,930 --> 00:05:56,830
How?

116
00:05:56,436 --> 00:05:58,789
We find out in viroids and prions.
